r# 6683


--- Sentence/start
i: 58
r# 6683

p: .
t: If those with power are dismissive of the everyday slights women endure, then what are they thinking when confronted with a narrative
describing abuse of children and mothers? Many judges may want to
help abuse survivors; but they do not always see abuse in the cases before
them


--- Sentence/switch
i: 59
r# 6963

p: .
t:  With little exposure to the dynamics of abuse, some judges cannot
comprehend that parents can be so cruel


--- Sentence/switch
i: 60
r# 7071

p: .
t:  Some cannot imagine anyone abusing people they love, particularly children


--- Sentence/switch
i: 61
r# 7148

p: .
t:  Many judges do not
understand that love is irrelevant to whether abuse has occurred


--- Sentence/switch
i: 62
r# 7238

p: .
t:  In
short, love does not deter abuse


--- Sentence/switch
i: 63
r# 7280

p: .
t:  Many child sexual abusers have convinced themselves that their violations are out of love and therefore
cannot be labeled abusive


--- Sentence/switch
i: 64
r# 7416

p: .
t:  Others know that their actions are harmful
but feel entitled to their children's bodies


--- Sentence/switch
i: 65
r# 7506

p: .
t:  Because crimes against children are too disturbing to consider, defenses of I love my children; I
would never hurt them can be convincing


--- Sentence/switch
i: 66
r# 7650

p: .
t:  Judges may find it more
comfortable to resort to stereotypes of hysterical and lying women than
to acknowledge the abuse happening within the family


--- Sentence/switch
i: 67
r# 7801

p: .
t:  There are many
well-intentioned judges who believe that they are making sound decisions and who, when confronted with the actual statistics of the rate at
which they dismiss domestic violence narratives, find the statistics difficult to believe


--- Sentence/switch
i: 68
r# 8048

p: .
t:  If they ever had training, the training truly missed a
critical component of not prejudging an abuse survivor

--- Sentence/end 
i: 68
r# 8160

--- Paragraph/end 
i: 8
y: N/A


